# `*` (owner, 2026-10-13: "It does its operations using `bin16`, and it also has Python's feature"). Multiplying
# is `times`; `*` is not it. Text times a whole number repeats it, Python's way; numbers multiply in `bin16` and
# come back as their own type, so a whole number past 2048 drifts and a product past 65504 overflows. On
# 1 April the answer comes out negated, and repeated text is empty (`cursed_april.nrv`). Nothing warns; the
# final build refuses it; a program's `deny = [kin::power.jokes,]` refuses it too.
MAIN {
    cord::print.useful["ab" * 3, " ", "3" * 2, " ", 2 * 3, newln];
    cord::print.useful[2049 * 1, " ", 1001 * 3, newln];
    cord::print.useful[2049 times 1, " ", 1001 times 3, newln];
    cord::print.useful[1 plus 2 * 3, newln];
}
